{{also|Gee|GEE|gée}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
A shortening of {{m|en|Jesus}}, perhaps as in the oath {{m|en|by Jesus}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/d͡ʒiː/}}
* {{rhymes|en|iː|s=1}}

====Interjection====
{{en-intj}}

# {{lb|en|somewhat|dated}} {{n-g|A general [[exclamation]] of [[surprise]] or [[frustration]].}}
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:wow}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Gee''', I didn't know that!}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Gee''', you didn't have to kick me!}}
#* {{quote-av|en|medium=motion picture|title={{w|Roberta (1935 film)|Roberta}}|year=1935|writers=Jane Murfin; Sam Mintz; Allan Scott (screenplay)|publisher=RKO Pictures|passage=Stephanie (''Irene Dunne''): Oh, yes. I like the English. And the Americans, too! / John Kent (''Randolph Scott''): '''Gee''', that's swell. I'm an American! / Stephanie: '''Gee''', that's swe–, I mean, I thought so.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|text=Every night when I start to sing that song, I think, “'''Gee''', what a song. What a moment.”|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/features/celine-dion-my-heart-will-go-on-oral-history-interview-7800213/|first=Mickey|last=Rapkin|date=2017-05-18|work=billboard.com|title=The Oral History of Celine Dion’s ‘My Heart Will Go On’: Controversies, Doubts & ‘Belly Pains’ In the Studio|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261002232723/https://www.billboard.com/music/features/celine-dion-my-heart-will-go-on-oral-history-interview-7800213/|archivedate=2 October 2026}}

=====Usage notes=====
''Gee'' is generally considered somewhat dated or juvenile. It is often used for ironic or sarcastic effect, with the speaker putting on an air of youthful innocence.

